    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d670-a1">

                <pb facs="0898" n="788" xml:id="pg788"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Minister in Portugal</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-06-21"><hi rendition="#small-caps">June</hi> 21,
                        1928</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I called on the Minister for Foreign Affairs this afternoon and discussed with
                    him the question of 10% shipping discrimination.</p>
                <p>The Minister said he was anxious to clear up this matter and that it had been
                    proposed to solve it by putting foreign and Portuguese ships on the same basis
                    but paying the Portuguese a subvention to offset their loss, but that in view of
                    the Finance Minister’s economy program it had been difficult to determine where
                    the money for the subvention was coming from. I indicated that such a solution
                    would seem to leave the foreign vessels right where they were, and said the
                    shipping interests were fighting it hard. He said the plan would apply only to
                    Portugal and the Adjacent Islands but that to open up the colonies to foreign
                    shipping would be to annihilate the Portuguese lines. I said I thought the
                    surest way to get the colonies developed would be to open them up and that the
                    indirect advantage of so doing would more than offset to the country the
                    shipping losses and that the country should have preference over a few
                    firms.</p>
                <closer>[<signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fred Morris
                    Dearing</hi></signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
